# 0012. The writer lock lives in the checkout

- Status: Proposed
- Date: 2026-11-25
- Amends: [0010](0010-filesystem-event-log.adr) (the unclean-writer interlock only)

The single-writer guarantee (ADR 0004) and the crash interlock (ADR 0010) are kept in the
transcript, under the data folder, and matched to checkouts by path. A checkout renamed
after a crash, or a second roundtable with another data folder (`XDG_DATA_HOME`), misses an
unfinished writer. This ADR moves the writer lock into the checkout itself.

## Technical details
- Where: `<git dir>/roundtable/` of the checkout, from `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`
  run in the working folder (a linked worktree has its own git dir, so each worktree is its
  own checkout). The git dir moves with the checkout and is the same for every roundtable
  on it, whatever its data folder. Nothing is written to the working tree.
- Only git checkouts can have a writer. Outside git every turn is read-only: no second,
  weaker locking scheme. If `GIT_DIR` or `GIT_WORK_TREE` is set in roundtable's
  environment, edits are locked too: two launches with different overrides could otherwise
  resolve one checkout to two lock directories. Git errors lock edits, as now.
- Two files:
  - `writer.lock`: an exclusive `flock` held for exactly one writable turn. The OS drops it
    when the process dies, so it means "a writer is running right now", across instances
    and data folders.
  - `writer.json`: the crash marker `{pid, started_ms, thread, data_root}`.
- Starting a writable turn, in this order, before the delivery is recorded as `sent`:
  1. take `writer.lock` (held: another roundtable is editing this checkout, refuse);
  2. check `writer.json` is absent (present: an earlier writer never finished, refuse and
     lock edits);
  3. write `writer.json`, sync it and sync the `roundtable/` directory (and the git dir the
     first time the folder is created), so the marker survives power loss.
  Any failure refuses the turn (fail closed); the delivery is then recorded `refused`, not
  `sent`, and the turn is shown failed. Cross-instance contention refuses rather than
  queues: the other instance's turn length is unknown, and the user can resend.
- Ending it: after the CLI is reaped and the reply recorded, remove `writer.json`, sync the
  directory, then release the lock.
- Startup: `writer.json` present while the lock is free means a writer crashed: edits are
  locked and the chat shows the marker. `/quiet` takes the lock, checks the marker is byte
  for byte the one that was shown (otherwise it shows the new one and asks again), removes
  it, syncs, and only then unlocks. It appends `settle interrupted` to the marker's thread
  when that thread is in this data folder; otherwise it records a `status` line in the
  current thread. An unreadable marker also locks; `/quiet` removes it after the same
  confirmation.
- The transcript keeps its `writable` delivery and reply lines as history, but they no
  longer decide anything: the transcript scan (`Store::writer_locks`) is removed.
- No migration: the transcript-based lock shipped the same day (PR #13); the owner's data
  folder was checked and has no open writer markers.
- Known limit: if the checkout is renamed *during* a writable turn and another checkout
  takes its old path, cleanup by path could touch the wrong marker. Pinning the directory
  needs `openat`; not worth it for this case.

Consequences: concurrent instances and crashes are covered per checkout, across data
folders and renames, with two small files. Cost: roundtable writes a subfolder into the git
dir; outside git nobody edits.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: std `File::try_lock`, `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`, serde_json
- Alternatives: keep the transcript scan and add a checkout id file: still two sources of truth; a data-root fallback outside git: a second, weaker scheme (Codex critique); a `.roundtable/` folder in the working tree: shows up in `git status` and needs ignoring; a global lock in the home folder keyed by path: breaks on rename, which is the case to fix
